<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Macedonia (region)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Macedonia is a historical region of the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, centred on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardar">Vardar</a> and Aliakmon valleys and the plain around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>. It takes its name from the ancient kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a> and later formed a Roman province, a contested borderland between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a>, and part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> until 1912–1913. It is now divided mainly between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a> and Bulgaria.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/macedonia-region.jpg" alt="Macedonia (region)" width="320" height="184" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Пакко · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Borders_of_Macedonia_according_authors_(1843-1927).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Macedonia is a geographical and historical region of the Balkan Peninsula in Southeast Europe. In the 19th century the name was revived for a region bounded by Mount Olympus, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindus">Pindus</a> range, the mountains Shar and Osogovo, the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhodope-mountains">Rhodopes</a>, the lower course of the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesta">Mesta</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean Sea</a>. The ancient kingdom of Macedonia, which gave the region its name, lay before the expansion of Philip II within the central and western parts of what is now Greek Macedonia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The earliest known settlements, such as Nea Nikomedeia in Imathia, date back about 9,000 years. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> ended Macedonian sovereignty in the 2nd century BCE: Philip V was defeated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-macedonian-war">Second Macedonian War</a>, and Perseus lost his kingdom in the Third. The land was divided into four districts and annexed as a Roman province in 146 BCE.</p><p>From the beginning of the 6th century Slavic tribes raided the Byzantine Balkan provinces, and great parts of Macedonia came to be controlled by Slavic-speaking communities, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a> held out. The parts ruled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a> in the 9th and 10th centuries were taken into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> in 1018. After the Ottoman victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-maritsa">Battle of Maritsa</a> in 1371 most of Macedonia accepted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassalage</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> took Thessalonica in 1430. The region remained Ottoman for nearly 500 years and became multicultural, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a>, Turks, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> and Aromanians among its peoples.</p><p>In the 19th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a> contested the region, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/internal-macedonian-revolutionary-organization">Internal Macedonian Revolutionary Organization</a> organised the Ilinden-Preobrazhenie Uprising in 1903. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-balkan-war">First Balkan War</a>, begun in August 1912, Greek forces entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> first. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> about 60,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from Thessaloniki were deported to extermination camps in occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The modern region at its maximum extent spans the largest part of Northern Greece, known as Greek Macedonia, all of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-macedonia">North Macedonia</a>, a large part of southwestern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, known as Pirin Macedonia, and small parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albania">Albania</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>. Greek Macedonia's largest city, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessaloniki</a>, is the second largest in Greece.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Macedonia (region)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They settled in new suburbs of Athens and Thessaloniki and on land in <b>Macedonia</b> distributed by agrarian reform.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Second Balkan War of 1913 Serbia, Greece, Romania, the Ottoman Empire and Montenegro fought Bulgaria over <b>Macedonia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-serbia">Kingdom of Serbia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The agrarian reform begun in 1919 abolished the remaining serf-like tenancies in Bosnia and <b>Macedonia</b> and redistributed large estates, but productivity stayed low and rural overpopulation severe; in 1921 about half the adult population was illiterate.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-yugoslavia">Kingdom of Yugoslavia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Anarchists took part in the Strandzha Commune and the Krusevo Republic of 1903 in <b>Macedonia</b> and in the Mexican Revolution of 1910.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/anarchism">Anarchism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>The Berziti were among the Slavic groups that settled in <b>Macedonia</b> in the 7th century, in the region around Ohrid, Prilep and Bitola.</blockquote><a href="/state/berziti">Berziti</a></li><li><blockquote>During the Marcomannic Wars, in about 170 CE, they broke through the Danube frontier, swept through Moesia, Thrace and <b>Macedonia</b>, and plundered the mysteries sanctuary at Eleusis near Athens.</blockquote><a href="/state/costoboci">Costoboci</a></li></ul></section>
